{"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":".error","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is the one state whose presentation can’t be fully generic — a timed-out request, a missing article, and a malformed response all deserve different words, and sometimes a different action, even though they’re all just an ","type":"text"},{"code":"Error","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" to ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","type":"reference"},{"text":". This article rounds up the three pieces ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorView","type":"reference"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorUIView","type":"reference"},{"text":" use to narrow that gap without you writing a fully custom placeholder.","type":"text"}]},{"text":"The offline-aware variant","anchor":"The-offline-aware-variant","type":"heading","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState\/isConnectivityError(_:)","type":"reference"},{"text":" is ","type":"text"},{"code":"true","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for a ","type":"text"},{"code":"URLError","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" indicating no network connectivity (","type":"text"},{"code":".notConnectedToInternet","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":".networkConnectionLost","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") or a timed-out request (","type":"text"},{"code":".timedOut","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":"). When it’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"true","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", the default Error placeholders swap their usual warning-triangle ico